    86	
    87	test (CUT) commonly suffers from. Intuitively, a larger test code
    88	base entails longer execution times and is more prone to defects.
    89	While a number of practical strategies exist for limiting the
    90	execution time of automated developer tests (e.g., regression test
    91	selection or deferring expensive tests to periodic “nightly” runs),
    92	the problem of erroneous test code has been repeatedly reported
    93	as a significant problem over the past decade, in particular in the
    94	form of _flaky tests_ . Flaky tests can non-deterministically pass or fail
    95	when repeatedly executed without any changes to the code and
    96	have been reported as a major impediment to regression testing
    97	and continuous integration efficacy (see [ 29 ] for an overview), because they can falsely indicate errors during development. When
    98	they do, developers may waste time hunting down bugs in their
    99	code that do not exist and are only believed to exist because of
   100	flaky tests’ spurious indication. Flaky tests are also problematic for
   101	debugging, because of their non-deterministic behavior. If a test
   102	fails consistently due to a error in the test code, the problem can
   103	be reproduced and the problematic test repaired. If, however, a test
   104	fails non-deterministically, repair success and time are affected by
   105	its (spurious) failure rate.
   106	To this end, previous research has proposed approaches for identifying and controlling factors that trigger flaky test failures (e.g.,
   107	
   108	[ 20, 31, 40 ]) and reported differing impact of these factors for different programming languages [ 6, 15, 17, 27 ]. Our study contributes
   109	to this effort by investigating the prevalence of test interference
   110	in C projects, in which test automation practices tend to differ significantly from the previously investigated languages in several
   111	aspects:
   112	
   113	  - There is no single commonly adopted test framework (cf.
   114	
   115	[ 30 ]) like JUnit for Java. As for Python, a number of different
   116	frameworks is being used, but the variety is larger [30].
   117	
   118	  - Unit tests are rare in C projects [ 19 ]. Most tests operate on
   119	the fully integrated software.
   120	
   121	  - Test executions are usually strongly isolated as individual
   122	processes [1], which restricts resource sharing across tests to
   123	shared system resources, such as files and sockets.
   124	
   125	  - From our observations, tests are mostly added to safeguard
   126	against previously encountered defects and regressions, rather
   127	than to optimize for structural coverage or mutation adequacy [2] .
   128	
   129	
   130	It is reasonable to assume that such differences have an effect on
   131	
   132	the prevalence of test interferences. For instance, tests that execute
   133	in isolated processes do not share heap memory by default, on
   134	
   135	
   136	1 This is probably due to the little overhead that process isolation entails compared to
   137	multi-threading [30].
   138	2 An illustrative example from our dataset: Test files like [tests/test-rules.c](https://github.com/VirusTotal/yara/blob/65feab41d4cbf4a75338561d8506fc1fa9fa6ba6/tests/test-rules.c) in
   139	commit 65feab41 of project VirusTotal/yara references numerous issues in the
   140	comments. Line coverage for this project has been measured as 0.68 with gcov, which
   141	is well above the median across all projects in our study (0.54), but also well below the
   142	median of the statement coverage reported for Java projects (0.78) [18].
   143	
   144	
   145	
   146	166
   147	
   148	
   149	ASE ’24, October 27-November 1, 2024, Sacramento, CA, USA Florian Eder and Stefan Winter
   150	
   151	
   152	
   153	which they could interfere. However, they do share _system state_,
   154	such as files in the shared file system, network sockets, etc.
   155	To study the prevalence of test interference in C projects on
   156	shared system state, we answer the following research questions in
   157	a large-scale study across 134 projects.
   158	
   159	
   160	**RQ 1:** **To which degree does tracking accesses to shared sys-**
   161	**tem state reduce interference detection overhead for**
   162	
   163	**C projects?**
   164	Test interference detection entails significant overheads, because all possibly interfering test sequences need to be executed. As test interference cannot occur _without_ accesses
   165	
   166	to shared resources, interference detection can be omitted
   167	in these cases. We propose and evaluate four dynamic detection mechanisms for shared resource accesses to limit
   168	
   169	interference detection overheads.
   170	
   171	**RQ 2: Do tests in C projects modify shared system state?**
   172	Existing work [ 30 ] has reported initial evidence that C tests
   173	may interfere on shared file system state based on static
   174	analyses. Based on this observation of potential interference
   175	_despite comparatively strong process isolation of tests_, we extend the detection to other types of possibly shared resources
   176	across processes, such as network sockets and the POSIX
   177	environment, and systematically assess system state modifications using dynamic analyses of test executions in a large
   178	set of C projects.
   179	**RQ 3:** **Do C projects contain tests that interfere on shared**
   180	**system state?**
   181	While system state modification is a _potential_ source of test
   182	interference, the modification alone is not sufficient, as we
   183	detail in Section 3.1. Hence, we investigate if test interference
   184	_manifests_ in deviating test results.
   185	**RQ 4:** **To which degree can projects without test interference**
   186	**benefit from parallel execution?**
   187	Test interference is not only detrimental to test result stability ( _order-dependent flaky tests_ [ 20, 44 ] can change their
   188	outcome if execution order is not controlled for). A related
   189	question (“How can C projects run tests in parallel without
   190	interference?”) has been addressed before [ 30 ]. Therefore,
   191	we investigate how the published results transport to the
   192	significantly larger set of subjects in our study.
   193	
   194	
   195	From our results, we conclude that (a) order-dependent flaky
   196	tests are less an issue for C projects than for Java or Python (only
   197	2 / 13 detected interferences), (b) concurrency-dependent flaky tests
   198	account for a significant fraction of detected interferences (11 / 13)
   199	in our study, (c) test result deviations in C projects often result from
   200	the use of default timeout values (13 / 26 cases), (d) test interferences
   201	in C projects occur despite relatively low test coverage (54 _._ 5 %
   202	line coverage in the median), and, maybe unsurprisingly, (e) test
   203	interference prevents test execution speedups from parallel test
   204	
   205	executions.
   206	
   207	Most importantly, we find our proposed reduction techniques
   208	for interference detection to offer significant savings, often down
   209	to individual test executions in isolation and 0 permutations to
   210	execute. The overheads that our analyses entail are amortized by
   211	the savings with up to 30 _._ 8 h (median 97 _._ 3 s) net savings for 112
   212	
   213	
   214	
   215	projects opposing up to 5 min (median 16 _._ 4 s) net overhead for the
   216	remaining 22 projects.
   217	
   218	
   219	**2** **BACKGROUND AND RELATED WORK**
   220	
   221	
   222	In the existing literature, test interference on shared state is mostly
   223	discussed as a root cause of flaky tests. Parry et al. provide a literature survey on the topic [ 29 ], which we highly recommend
   224	for an overview. While not all flaky tests are caused by interference on shared state (e.g., deterministic implementations of nondeterministic specifications [ 31 ]), the majority are according to
   225	empirical results [ 15, 27 ]. Our notion of shared system state comprises all system resources that tests share, such as files, network
   226	sockets, and the available CPU time. In the following we use the
   227	terms _shared state_ and _shared resource_ synonymously, as access to a
   228	shared resource implies access to shared system state and access to
   229	shared system state implies access to one or more shared resources.
   230	
   231	
   232	**2.1** **Empirical Studies**
   233	
   234	
   235	In a study of flakiness-fixing commits in Apache projects, Luo et al.
   236	
   237	[ 27 ] identified 10 recurring root causes for flaky tests, half of which
   238	involve shared resource access: Concurrency describes interfering
   239	access to shared resources in parallel, interleaving test executions.
   240	Test Order Dependency describes interfering access to shared
   241	resources in sequential execution. Async Wait describes situations
   242	when the results of asynchronous calls is not properly waited for.
   243	The reason why tests do not _consistently_ fail in these cases is that
   244	execution times for the caller and callee vary depending on waiting time for shared resource access (CPU, locks, etc.). Network
   245	describes both unavailability of a remote resource and bad management of local sockets. The latter covers interference on shared
   246	sockets. IO describes possibly interfering I/O operations (e.g., file
   247	I/O). In sum, the identified root causes involving resource dependencies account for 139/201 inspected flakiness-fixing commits (i.e.,
   248	69 %) in the study. While their study targeted projects with different
   249	programming languages, the majority of the projects are written in
   250	Java and the reported C/C++ portions mostly cover auxiliary code.
   251	Except for two projects that only contain C code (APR, SpamAssassin), it is unclear whether the studied flakiness-fixing commits
   252	involve test code or CUT written in C.
   253	
   254	In a subsequent study that focused on Python projects, Gruber
   255	et al. [ 15 ] confirmed the prevalence of many root causes that were
   256	identified by Luo et al. for Java projects. However, they found their
   257	relative frequency to differ. According to their results, Test Order
   258	Dependency accounts for an impressive fraction of 59 % of the
   259	identified root causes (4461/7571 tests). That is, the majority of
   260	flaky tests in their study were found to involve test interference.
   261	While the authors also identified additional (non-order-dependent)
   262	tests with root causes that potentially involve resource interference
   263	(Network, IO, Async Wait, Concurrency), their contribution to
   264	the prevalence of interference based flaky tests is minor compared
   265	to the bulk of order-dependent tests.
   266	
   267	
   268	**2.2** **Detection Strategies**
   269	
   270	
   271	The detection strategies for flaky tests followed in existing work are
   272	mostly based on repeated executions, possibly under varying execution conditions. These conditions commonly correspond to the
   273	
   274	
   275	
   276	167
   277	
   278	
   279	Efficient Detection of Test Interference in C Projects ASE ’24, October 27-November 1, 2024, Sacramento, CA, USA
   280	
   281	
   282	
   283	hypothesized root causes of flaky tests. To detect order-dependent
   284	flaky tests, the execution order of tests within a test suite is changed
   285	and a number of different strategies to obtain test suite permutations
   286	to test with has been explored in existing research [ 20, 25, 41, 44 ].
   287	The reduction strategies we explore in this paper are orthogonal to
   288	the previously explored prioritization techniques for the detection
   289	of test order dependencies. By reducing the number of possibly
   290	interfering tests, our strategies reduce the number of relevant tests
   291	to consider in test permutations (entire test suite permutations,
   292	based on Tuscan squares or not, or pairwise permutations).
   293	The detection of Concurrency related execution interference
   294	
   295	is covered in extensive work on concurrency testing; see [ 1, 11 ] for
   296	an overview. In contrast to this existing work, our focus is on _test_
   297	_interference_, i.e., specific executions of a CUT that are triggered by
   298	developer tests, rather than finding all/most possible execution interferences in a project. As noise injection [ 26 ] has been reported to
   299	be effective for stimulating the generation of diverse CPU schedules
   300	and has consequently been proposed for the detection of related
   301	flaky tests [33, 39], we employ a similar technique in our work.
   302	An additional motivation to identify test interference is execution efficiency, as non-interfering tests can be safely executed in
   303	parallel. Schwahn et al. [ 30 ] investigated the interference of tests in
   304	C projects using a static data-flow analysis. Unlike their approach,
   305	we identify test interference using dynamic analyses, which do not
   306	depend on a certain compiler tool chain that possibly deviates from
   307	what developers are using. As our approach is significantly less
   308	intrusive, we consider it more widely applicable as witnessed by the
   309	much larger number of subjects we are able to cover in our study.
   310	However, the main motivation for our work is the identification
   311	of test interference as _defects in test code_ in contrast to _minimizing_
   312	_regression test latencies_ in their work. This allows us to use dynamic
   313	analyses, as execution efficiency considerations are of a secondary
   314	concern and can be analyzed “on hindsight”, as we do for RQ4.
   315	Moreover, our study reveals cases for which a dynamic analysis is
   316	favorable over a static analysis, because it (a) does not depend on
